I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided tungstate/GSAS-II guidance first. Then I’ll index the pattern, identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II, and verify every submitted artifact against the final project.

The chemistry is a low-temperature BaCO₃ + WO₃ reaction, so the first hypothesis set is unreacted BaCO₃/WO₃ plus possible BaWO₄ and low-temperature intermediates. I’m now extracting peak positions and matching them against the frozen COD library before committing any phases.

The peak inventory strongly supports an unreacted-precursor mixture: the characteristic split WO₃ cluster near 23–25° and the full witherite BaCO₃ series recur together through high angle. I do not yet see an independent BaWO₄ signature; I’ll test it quantitatively as an optional phase rather than infer product formation from chemistry alone.

The two-phase refinement is converging and already explains the pattern substantially better than the raw references. The refined masses remain near a 1:1 molar precursor blend; the main current issue is separating the angular zero from specimen displacement, which are intrinsically highly correlated in this geometry.

The candidate-phase tests return only trace signed scales (BaWO₄ ≈0.4 wt% and other Ba–W–O candidates ≤0.2 wt%) with negligible fit improvement and no independent peaks. I’m treating those as residual-model leakage, not evidence for additional crystalline phases, and will refine the two-phase model’s texture and atomic coordinates next.

The profile-effect screens show no convincing WO₃ texture, anisotropic strain, polarization, or roughness term; each changes Rwp by only ~0.01–0.03 percentage points, and roughness refines to an unphysical value. I’m keeping the simpler isotropic model and checking the remaining monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ ambiguity, which is the dominant structural uncertainty.

The triclinic WO₃ alternative does not resolve the ambiguity: it gives slightly better individual reflection intensities but a worse whole-profile fit and requires many more independent coordinates. I’m completing the coordinate test, then I’ll retain the simpler monoclinic model unless the improvement is decisive and physically plausible.

The phase assignment is now stable: BaCO₃ (witherite) plus monoclinic WO₃, with no defensible third crystalline phase. I’m selecting the least-perturbed structural refinement that retains the profile improvement, then I’ll replay the saved GSAS-II project and generate every submitted file from that same replay.

The first combined refinement lowers \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) to about 7.01%, but it inherited an over-correlated peak-shape solution outside the experiment’s calibrated parameter ranges. I’m re-refining from the same two structures with explicit physical limits on zero/height, instrumental width, size, strain, polarization, and texture; this is important because otherwise phase fractions and atomic shifts can absorb profile errors.

The bounded runs confirm that the angular zero and specimen-height correction are almost perfectly correlated, as expected for one Bragg–Brentano scan. One physically bounded start has already recovered a 7.77% fit before width refinement; I’m letting the width stages finish and will retain the solution with valid parameters rather than the numerically lowest but nonphysical decomposition.

Allowing a physically bounded low-angle surface correction produces a substantial improvement (about 5.93% after the first joint cycle), unlike the earlier unconstrained roughness test that ran to an impossible negative value. I’m checking whether that improvement survives a second cycle and whether the refined roughness lies inside its admissible range.

After forcing a true recalculation, the apparent 5.8% fit was partly a boundary-clamping artifact; the replay-consistent value is about 6.67%. I’ve corrected for that. The coordinate perturbations are now capped at the laboratory model’s allowed RMS displacements, and I’m doing the final nuisance-parameter cleanup with boundary terms fixed so the saved project will reproduce its reported profile.

The saved model now replays exactly: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=5.898\%\), \(R_p=4.492\%\), with no negative background, peak-width, polarization, or ADP flags. Replay gives 55.00 wt% BaCO₃ and 45.00 wt% WO₃; I’m exporting the two CIFs and then checking their symmetry, chemistry, interatomic distances, and project/CIF consistency.

Analysis complete. The crystalline assemblage is:

- Witherite BaCO₃: 55.00 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 45.00 wt%

Fresh GSAS-II replay gives \(R_\mathrm{wp}=5.898\%\) and \(R_p=4.492\%\), with no physical-review flags. Estimated systematic fraction uncertainty is about ±2 percentage points; trace phases below roughly 0.5–1 wt% and amorphous material cannot be quantified reliably.

